---
title: A bespoke autodiff engine
summary: Why skoro differentiates with its own forward/VJP/HVP engine instead of burn's autodiff backends.
order: 1
---

## Context

Inference algorithms for probabilistic models need more than a gradient. Hamiltonian Monte Carlo,
extended Kalman filters, and MAP estimation with Fisher information need Jacobians,
vector–Jacobian products, and **second-order** information (Hessian–vector products), and they
need them **with respect to quantities on non-linear manifolds**, differentiated in tangent
coordinates. burn provides reverse-mode autodiff as a backend decorator (`Autodiff<B>`).

## Decision

Skoro uses burn strictly for tensor execution and never wraps a backend in `Autodiff`. Every
operation implements `DiffOperation` (`forward`, `vjp`, `hvp`) by hand, and skoro's own tape runs
the passes over a compiled sequence.

## Consequences

- First- and second-order derivatives come from one mechanism, over one compiled program.
- Manifold-valued inputs are differentiated in their tangent spaces by construction.
- Every new operation costs a hand-written VJP and HVP, and those are where bugs live. The
  `skoro-diff-operation` skill and numerical checks exist to contain that cost.

